  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 325 AM EDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nWeekend conditions remain uncertain with high model variability in \nthe ejection of a mid level shortwave out of the southwest. \nPrecipitation output from this system has trended both later (more \ntowards late Sunday to early Monday) and with less QPF. The main \nmodes of variability are associated with the low which is currently \nbeing cut-off over the southwest coast. Models notoriously have a \ndifficult time resolving cut-off low movement and the GFS trends are \nlikely moving in the right, slower, direction. What this means for \nthe southeast is more time for the polar ridge to move overhead, \ncountering some of the lift potential going into the late weekend.\n\nAnother factor of import will be where the southern terminating edge \nof the near surface high ends. This will determine where PWATs drop \noff and could act as a focal point for any precipitation. This \neffect can be seen when comparing PWATs for the GFS and ECMWF. A \nmore southerly boundary of the high means low level easterly flow \ndeveloped CAD may limit moisture more towards PWATs of 1\" rather \nthan 1.5\"+.\n\nTemperatures are likely to continue their downtrend as we get into \nthe Sunday-Monday timeframe. CAD will reduce highs, though we will \nlikely still reach the 60s with lower moisture. Conditions rewarm \ninto the 70s as the shortwave passes and high pressure redevelops \ninto mid next week.",
